    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "More than 23.5 million Americans suffer from an autoimmune disease, which results when the immune system incorrectly identifies and attacks healthy tissue. Regulatory T cells (Tregs) represent a small subpopulation of T cells that play an important role in dampening self-reactive immune responses, and therefore offer an enticing target for a new generation of autoimmune therapies. Although it is generally understood that Tregs downregulate induction and proliferation of effector T cells, there is a lack of understanding about the specific mechanism of action of Treg cells. This has been compounded by the fact that very few peptides have been discovered that are known to elicit a Treg response. We therefore sought to identify and characterize peptides recognized by Tregs. We chose to work with Tregs found in the visceral adipose tissue of mice. These Tregs play a role in diabetes, which is a commonly used model of autoimmune disease.  Amplified proliferation of VAT-Tregs is associated with improved prognosis in a diabetes mouse model . To find the endogenous peptides, we have developed a new algorithm that can propose candidate peptides that bind a specific TCR by combining three data sets:  the deep sequence data from the affinity-based selection, T cell stimulation data of the most frequently occurring peptides from the affinity selection, and the BLOSUM62 matrix which is used to calculate amino-acid homology. The algorithm combines these three data sets to systematically analyze an inputted organism’s proteome to find probable candidate peptides.  Using the algorithm, we identified and confirmed four peptides from the mouse proteome that stimulated the VAT-TCR. With these new tools to aid in identifying peptides recognized by Tregs, we can now design experiments to enhance our understanding of Treg function and regulation, develop proximate translational applications to autoimmune diseases, and ultimately develop a new class of antigen specific therapies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
